<p>President Trump signed a May 1 executive order expanding U.S. sanctions on Cuba, targeting people and entities linked to broad sectors of the Cuban economy and warning foreign banks and companies not to deal with sanctioned targets. The White House said the measures target people, entities and affiliates that support Cuba’s security apparatus, engage in corruption or commit serious human rights violations. Cuba rejected the measures as “collective punishment” and “unilateral coercive measures.” Foreign Minister Bruno Rodríguez said Washington had no right to impose measures against Cuba or third countries. The sanctions were announced as Cuba held International Workers’ Day marches in Havana, including a procession near the U.S. Embassy where participants vowed to “defend the homeland”.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ELchu2rNEfECrKDxImnYljH0BHR03zht7iusuxSjO6_eb_IMyC_8ZAj3L9hcC89Yycjlahs0iLMRYmuch7VesoIUcMOZ_zgfZmDcO08ZXL-vasvW7gqBCGL3ODeDvtl8GdOAJdyzP3tFes1Tjiypq55oRVpgSW5SaGCnHa-r6u8rGarg-Wo_rwV9Qgvh87HjDDfGOmBRGkxjg4bhHsDY4Y0yn8SlE8eaWVGqpzi8uQ3dMdPLvMF8Jc8QJ3iL9Yklz2SHKBcXia_8dEhPxA' alt='People march along Havana&#x27;s waterfront to mark International Workers&#x27; Day at the Anti-Imperialist Platform in front of the U.S. Embassy in Havana on Friday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Regional backdrop: Reports based on Reuters said the sanctions came as Trump sought to increase pressure on Havana after ousting Venezuela’s Nicolás Maduro, placing Cuba within a wider regional confrontation.</li><li>Targeted sectors: The listed economic areas include energy, defense and related materiel, metals and mining, financial services and security.</li><li>March leaders: Ninety-four-year-old Raúl Castro joined thousands of Cubans in the May Day march along Havana’s waterfront with President Miguel Díaz-Canel.</li><li>Travel curbs: Reports said people listed under the order would be barred from visiting the United States, in addition to facing financial restrictions.</li></ul><blockquote>We firmly reject the recent unilateral coercive measures adopted - Bruno Rodríguez</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Cuban Foreign Minister Bruno Rodríguez: He called the measures illegal and abusive and said Havana would not be intimidated. (<a href='https://www.24chasa.bg/mezhdunarodni/article/22772822'>24 Chasa</a>, <a href='https://tocka.com.mk/vesti/554788/tramp-vovede-novi-sankcii-protiv-kuba-havana-vozvrati-nema-da-n-zaplasite'>Točka</a>)</li><li>President Miguel Díaz-Canel: He described the U.S. blockade as genocidal and said honest people could not accept the argument that Cuba threatens the United States. (<a href='https://www.nacion.com/el-mundo/trump-endurece-sanciones-a-cuba-en-plena-marcha/BNKUGCBMS5ABBLGM5LF3XOS5TE/story/'>La Nación</a>)</li><li>White House officials: Two officials confirmed the order, and reports said it was not immediately clear which people or entities were initially covered. (<a href='https://jovempan.com.br/noticias/mundo/trump-amplia-sancoes-dos-eua-contra-o-governo-cubano.html'>Jovem Pan</a>, <a href='https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/mundo/2026/05/01/trump-lanza-nueva-ronda-de-sanciones-contra-aparato-de-seguridad-del-regimen-cubano/'>El Financiero</a>)</li><li>President Trump: He said Cuba continues to represent an extraordinary threat to U.S. national security and raised the possibility of sending a U.S. aircraft carrier close to the island’s coast. (<a href='https://www.nacion.com/el-mundo/trump-endurece-sanciones-a-cuba-en-plena-marcha/BNKUGCBMS5ABBLGM5LF3XOS5TE/story/'>La Nación</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/us-sanctions-are-collective-punishment-says-cuba-during-may-1-marches/article70930842.ece'>U.S. sanctions are ‘collective punishment’, says Cuba during May 1 marches</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/05/01/cuba-calls-us-sanctions-collective-punishment-during-may-1-marc